A host's keyed packages evaluate each module once, not once an extension.

A keyed package's extensions already share one factory each across
folders, but each extension's import starts a module cache of its own, so
a library two of its extensions import is evaluated once for each. A
keyed package's loads now read and write Node's module cache, so each of
its files is evaluated once for the host. Every other package's loads
keep their modules their own. What a keyed package's loads left in the
cache goes at a reload, when a link moves off its copy, and when a load
begun before a reload ends, so the next load reads its files as they are.

